"B2":[
{id:"b2-1", sarlavha:"Remote work: a blessing or a problem?",
 matn:"Before 2020, working from home was considered a rare privilege. Many managers believed that employees who could not be seen at their desks were probably not working. The pandemic changed that view almost overnight. Companies that had never tested remote arrangements suddenly had to rely on video calls, shared documents and messaging apps. Surprisingly, many discovered that output did not fall. Some even reported higher productivity, mainly because staff spent less time commuting and fewer hours in unnecessary meetings.\n\nHowever, the picture is more complicated. Newcomers often find it hard to learn informally from colleagues, and teams can drift apart when nobody meets face to face. Many companies are now experimenting with hybrid models, in which employees come to the office for a few days a week. The challenge is no longer whether remote work is possible, but how to design it so that people stay connected and motivated.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What was the common belief about remote work before 2020?", togri:"Employees who worked from home were probably not working", chalg:["It was the most productive way to work","It was allowed only for managers","It was illegal in most countries"], izoh:"Matnning birinchi qismida ko'pchilik menejerlar uydan ishlovchilar ehtimol ishlamayapti deb o'ylagani aytiladi."},
  {s:"According to the text, why did some companies report higher productivity?", togri:"Employees had less travel time and fewer unnecessary meetings", chalg:["Employees worked longer hours","They hired more staff","Clients demanded faster results"], izoh:"Matnda sabab aniq aytilgan: yo'lda kam vaqt va keraksiz yig'ilishlar kamaygan."},
  {s:"What main challenge does the text identify for remote work now?", togri:"Designing it so that people stay connected and motivated", chalg:["Finding enough video software","Paying employees less","Returning everyone to offices full-time"], izoh:"Matn oxirida asosiy savol masofaviy ishni odamlar bog'langan va motivatsiyali bo'lishi uchun qanday loyihalash ekani deyilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-2", sarlavha:"The shrinking Aral Sea",
 matn:"The Aral Sea, once the fourth-largest lake in the world, has shrunk to a fraction of its former size. The main cause was the diversion of the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers to irrigate cotton fields during the Soviet period. Water that once flowed into the sea was channelled into canals and farmland. The sea slowly retreated, leaving behind salty deserts whose dust and chemicals are carried across the region.\n\nToday, governments in the region are trying to reverse some of the damage. Kazakhstan built a dam to protect the northern part of the sea, and its water level has partly recovered. Uzbekistan is working to modernise irrigation, replacing open canals with pipes and drip systems that lose far less water to evaporation. Experts agree that these measures help, but they warn that the southern basin is unlikely to return to its former state, because the water does not arrive in the same quantity.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"Why did the Aral Sea shrink?", togri:"Rivers were diverted to irrigate farmland", chalg:["Earthquakes destroyed its shores","It became a climate tourism site","Mining drained the water"], izoh:"Matnda sabab aniq aytilgan: Amudaryo va Sirdaryo suvi paxta dalalarini sug'orish uchun burilgan."},
  {s:"What did Kazakhstan do?", togri:"It built a dam to protect the northern part of the sea", chalg:["It stopped all cotton farming","It moved the rivers to another region","It drained the southern basin"], izoh:"Qozog'iston to'g'on qurgani va shimoliy qismda suv sathi qisman tiklangani aytilgan."},
  {s:"Why are experts doubtful that the southern basin will return to its former state?", togri:"The water does not arrive in the same quantity", chalg:["The fish stock has disappeared completely","The government banned irrigation","The dust storms have stopped"], izoh:"Matn oxirida suv avvalgidek miqdorda kelmayotgani uchun qaytish dargumon deyilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-3", sarlavha:"Sleep and memory",
 matn:"Many students believe that the best way to prepare for an exam is to study late into the night. Researchers who study memory suggest that this strategy often backfires. During sleep, the brain does not simply rest. It replays and reorganises the information gathered during the day. Connections between new facts and existing knowledge are strengthened, while irrelevant details tend to fade.\n\nIn one well-known type of experiment, participants learned a list of word pairs. Those who slept after learning remembered significantly more of them the next morning than those who stayed awake for the same period. This does not mean that sleep can replace study. Rather, it means that studying and then sleeping is more effective than studying and staying up. A sensible routine, therefore, is to review material in the evening and protect at least seven to eight hours of sleep.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"According to the text, what does the brain do during sleep?", togri:"It replays and reorganises the information gathered during the day", chalg:["It stops working completely","It forgets everything learned that day","It prepares new study materials"], izoh:"Matnda uyquda miya kun davomida olingan ma'lumotlarni qayta ijro etib, tartibga solishi aytilgan."},
  {s:"What did the word-pair experiment show?", togri:"Those who slept remembered more the next morning", chalg:["Sleepers forgot less, but learned nothing new","Awake participants learned faster","Sleep had no connection with memory"], izoh:"Uxlagan ishtirokchilar ertasi kuni ko'proq so'z juftini esladi."},
  {s:"What is the main advice of the text?", togri:"Review in the evening and protect your sleep", chalg:["Replace study with sleep","Avoid reviewing before an exam","Study only in the morning"], izoh:"Matn oxirida kechqurun takrorlash va yetarli uyqu tavsiya qilingan."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-4", sarlavha:"The Friday night market",
 matn:"Every Friday evening, a narrow street in the old quarter of the city turns into a night market. By seven o'clock, the first stalls are already selling grilled corn, sweet tea and handmade ceramics. Visitors who arrive early can watch the vendors set up their tables under strings of lights, while local families stroll slowly from one stall to the next.\n\nWhat makes the market special is not only the food but the atmosphere. Musicians often play near the entrance, and older craftsmen demonstrate techniques that their grandparents taught them. Prices are generally fair, though bargaining is expected for larger items. The best advice is to come hungry, arrive before nine, when the crowds are thinner, and leave some space in your bag for a souvenir.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What time should visitors arrive to avoid the biggest crowds?", togri:"Before nine o'clock", chalg:["Before seven o'clock","After midnight","At noon"], izoh:"Matn oxirida odamlar kamroq bo'lgan to'qqizgacha kelish maslahat berilgan."},
  {s:"What, according to the text, makes the market special?", togri:"Its atmosphere, music and craft demonstrations", chalg:["Only its low prices","Imported products","Large shopping malls nearby"], izoh:"Matnda muhit, musiqachilar va hunarmandlar namoyishi ta'kidlangan."},
  {s:"What does the writer say about larger items?", togri:"Bargaining is expected when buying them", chalg:["They should be bought immediately","Visitors should avoid them","They always cost double"], izoh:"Matnda katta buyumlar uchun savdolashish odatiy hol ekani aytilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-5", sarlavha:"Don't be afraid of mistakes",
 matn:"Adults learning a foreign language often feel embarrassed by their mistakes. They avoid speaking in case they sound foolish, and as a result they progress more slowly than their more daring classmates. Teachers have long known that errors are not simply failures. They are evidence that the learner is testing a hypothesis about how the language works.\n\nConsider a learner who says \"I goed there yesterday.\" The mistake shows that the learner has noticed the regular past tense pattern but has not yet mastered the irregular verb \"go\". Correcting the form is useful, but the most important step is to keep communicating. Learners who speak frequently, even imperfectly, tend to develop fluency faster than those who wait until they are sure of every rule.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"Why do many adult learners progress slowly, according to the text?", togri:"They avoid speaking because they are embarrassed", chalg:["They lack intelligence","Teachers ignore them","The language is too easy for them"], izoh:"Matnda kattalar uyalib, gapirishdan qochgani sekin rivojlanishning sababi sifatida aytilgan."},
  {s:"What does the example \"I goed there yesterday\" show?", togri:"The learner noticed the past tense pattern but not the irregular verb", chalg:["The learner knows nothing about English","The learner speaks perfectly","The learner should stop studying"], izoh:"O'quvchi -ed qolipini payqagan, ammo go fe'lining noqoida shaklini hali bilmaydi."},
  {s:"According to the text, what is the most important step?", togri:"Keep communicating", chalg:["Memorise all the rules first","Avoid being corrected","Only practise writing"], izoh:"Matn oxirida muloqotni davom ettirish eng muhim qadam deyilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-6", sarlavha:"The economics of street food",
 matn:"Street food is often dismissed as cheap, improvised and unhygienic, but economists increasingly see it as a serious source of income. In many developing cities, street vendors support whole families, and their small businesses need almost no bank loans. A vendor with a cart can start work with a few hundred dollars, buy ingredients every day and keep cash flowing without storing large stocks.\n\nThe same informality that makes street food attractive also creates problems. Vendors may lack licences, face sudden evictions, or pay informal fees to local officials. Some cities have responded by creating designated food zones with water supply and waste collection. Such schemes improve safety and reduce conflict. Critics, however, argue that they can also push out the poorest sellers, who cannot afford the new permits.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"How do economists increasingly see street food?", togri:"As a serious source of income", chalg:["As a hobby","As a health risk only","As a tourist trap"], izoh:"Matnda iqtisodchilar ko'cha ovqatini jiddiy daromad manbai deb ko'rishi aytilgan."},
  {s:"Why do street vendors need few bank loans?", togri:"They start small and buy ingredients every day", chalg:["They have no customers","Banks have forbidden them to borrow","The government pays their costs"], izoh:"Sotuvchilar kichik kapital bilan boshlab, har kuni mahsulot sotib olishi aytilgan."},
  {s:"What criticism of food zones does the text mention?", togri:"They may push out the poorest sellers", chalg:["They are too cheap for vendors","They have no water supply","They increase crime"], izoh:"Tanqidchilar yangi ruxsatnomalarni to'lay olmaydigan eng kambag'al sotuvchilar chetga surilishini aytadi."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-7", sarlavha:"Samarkand: a crossroads of history",
 matn:"Samarkand has been a meeting point for merchants, scholars and conquerors for more than two thousand years. Its position on the Silk Road allowed it to grow rich from trade in silk and spices, and the city became known for its observatories, libraries and madrasas. In the fifteenth century, the astronomer Ulugbek built an observatory whose instruments measured the positions of stars with remarkable precision for their time.\n\nToday, visitors come to admire the Registan, a group of three majestic madrasas whose tiles shine in the afternoon sun. Yet the city's importance was not only architectural. Its scholars exchanged ideas with partners across Asia, and their work influenced mathematics and astronomy far beyond the region. Historians often describe Samarkand as a reminder that cultural achievement tends to flourish where different peoples meet and share knowledge.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What made Samarkand rich, according to the text?", togri:"Its position on the Silk Road", chalg:["Gold mines","Oil reserves","Fishing"], izoh:"Matnda shaharning Ipak yo'lidagi joylashuvi savdo orqali boylik keltirgani aytilgan."},
  {s:"What did Ulugbek build?", togri:"An observatory", chalg:["A palace","A railway","A harbour"], izoh:"Matnda astronom Ulug'bek XV asrda rasadxona qurgani aytiladi."},
  {s:"What does the text suggest about cultural achievement?", togri:"It flourishes where different peoples meet and share knowledge", chalg:["It develops best in isolation","It is purely architectural","It declines when trade grows"], izoh:"Tarixchilar fikriga ko'ra, madaniy yutuqlar turli xalqlar uchrashib, bilim almashgan joyda gullaydi."}
 ]},
{id:"b2-8", sarlavha:"Attention and the phone",
 matn:"Many people now check their phones dozens of times a day, often without a clear purpose. Psychologists describe this as a habit loop: a cue, a routine and a reward. Each notification offers a small, unpredictable reward, and the brain learns to expect it. Over time, the habit can make it harder to concentrate on tasks that require sustained effort, such as reading a long article or solving a complex problem.\n\nSome researchers argue that the effect is exaggerated, and that the real problem is how people use their time online rather than how often they check their phones. Others suggest simple countermeasures: turning off non-essential notifications, keeping the phone out of the bedroom and scheduling specific times for messages. Whatever the scientific verdict, most people agree that a little more control over attention would improve their daily lives.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What does a habit loop consist of, according to the text?", togri:"A cue, a routine and a reward", chalg:["Sleep, work and rest","Input, output and error","A question, an answer and a review"], izoh:"Matnda odat halqasi: signal, odat va mukofotdan iborat ekani aytilgan."},
  {s:"What do some researchers argue?", togri:"The effect may be exaggerated, and how people use their time matters more", chalg:["Checking the phone has no effect at all","Phones should be banned in schools","Notifications are essential"], izoh:"Ba'zi tadqiqotchilar ta'sirni bo'rttirilgan deb hisoblashi aytilgan."},
  {s:"Which countermeasure does the text suggest?", togri:"Turning off non-essential notifications", chalg:["Buying a new phone","Working at night","Keeping the phone in the bedroom"], izoh:"Matnda zarur bo'lmagan bildirishnomalarni o'chirish taklif qilingan."}
 ]}
],
"C1":[
{id:"c1-1", sarlavha:"The paradox of efficiency",
 matn:"Efficiency is usually presented as an unqualified good. A factory that produces more with less, a hospital that shortens waiting times, a city that moves commuters faster: each appears to be a clear success. Yet the economist William Stanley Jevons observed in the nineteenth century that improvements in the efficiency of coal use led not to lower consumption but to greater consumption, since cheaper coal opened up new uses. The phenomenon, now called the rebound effect, is far from confined to coal.\n\nWhen a road is widened to relieve congestion, it often attracts new drivers who had previously avoided travelling at peak times, and within a few years the traffic returns. The lesson is not that efficiency is worthless, but that it rarely solves a problem on its own. Unless the underlying demand is understood, gains in efficiency may simply be absorbed by the very behaviour they were meant to curb.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What is the main point of the passage?", togri:"Efficiency gains can increase total consumption", chalg:["Efficiency is always harmful","Roads should never be widened","Coal use should be banned"], izoh:"Matn Jevons paradoksi va qaytish effektini tasvirlab, samaradorlik iste'molni oshirishi mumkinligini ko'rsatadi."},
  {s:"The word \"unqualified\" in the first sentence most nearly means:", togri:"Absolute, without reservations", chalg:["Partial","Unlikely","Hidden"], izoh:"Unqualified — hech qanday shartsiz, to'liq degan ma'noni anglatadi."},
  {s:"What does the author imply about demand?", togri:"It is often underestimated when efficiency improves", chalg:["It stays constant","It disappears with new technology","It only affects coal"], izoh:"Muallif asosiy talab tushunilmasa, samaradorlik foydasi o'sha xulq-atvorga singib ketishini ta'kidlaydi."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-2", sarlavha:"Language change and prestige",
 matn:"Linguists have long noticed that the speech of prestigious groups is imitated by those who aspire to their status. Yet the direction of influence is not always downward, from elite to ordinary speakers. Some features spread from working-class communities into mainstream speech, and are then adopted by the very people who once looked down on them. Such changes reveal that prestige is not a fixed property of a dialect but a social judgement that can shift over time.\n\nConsequently, labelling a pronunciation \"careless\" or a grammatical pattern \"incorrect\" tells us more about the speaker's social assumptions than about the language itself. Standard varieties are, after all, dialects that happened to gain political and economic backing. Recognising this does not mean abandoning standard forms, which remain useful for formal writing and wide communication. It does, however, invite a more humble attitude towards the speech of others.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What does the author suggest about prestige?", togri:"It is a social judgement that can shift over time", chalg:["It is fixed and permanent","It belongs only to elites","It cannot spread between groups"], izoh:"Matnda nufuz o'zgarmas xususiyat emas, balki siljishi mumkin bo'lgan ijtimoiy hukm ekani aytilgan."},
  {s:"Why are standard varieties described as dialects \"that happened to gain backing\"?", togri:"To show that their status comes from social and political factors, not inherent superiority", chalg:["To mock standard speakers","To say they are simply errors","To claim they are recent inventions"], izoh:"Muallif standart shakl maqomi ijtimoiy-siyosiy omillardan kelganini ta'kidlaydi."},
  {s:"What is the author's attitude to standard forms?", togri:"They are useful, but they call for humility towards others' speech", chalg:["They should be abolished","He is indifferent to them","He is eager to abandon them"], izoh:"Muallif standart shakllarning foydaliligini tan oladi, lekin boshqalar nutqiga kamtarona munosabatni tavsiya qiladi."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-3", sarlavha:"Urban heat islands",
 matn:"Cities are typically several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island effect. Asphalt and concrete absorb solar radiation during the day and release it slowly after dark, while the dense arrangement of buildings reduces the airflow that might otherwise cool the streets. Vegetation, which cools the air through evapotranspiration, is often scarce in precisely the districts where it is most needed.\n\nThe consequences are unevenly distributed. Lower-income neighbourhoods frequently have fewer trees, older housing without air conditioning and more exposure to traffic, so residents there bear a disproportionate share of heat-related illness. Planners have increasingly responded with green roofs, reflective pavements and tree-planting programmes. Such measures are not merely cosmetic. They address a risk that is both environmental and, in a real sense, a question of social justice.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"Why are cities warmer than the countryside?", togri:"Concrete absorbs heat and dense buildings reduce airflow", chalg:["They have less industry","They receive more rainfall","They have fewer people"], izoh:"Matnda beton va zich binolar issiqlik orolining asosiy sabablari sifatida aytilgan."},
  {s:"What does the author imply about the risk of heat?", togri:"Lower-income residents bear a disproportionate share of it", chalg:["It affects everyone equally","It only affects tourists","It is purely a cosmetic problem"], izoh:"Matnda kam daromadli tumanlarda issiqlik bilan bog'liq kasalliklar nomutanosib ko'pligi aytilgan."},
  {s:"What does the phrase \"not merely cosmetic\" suggest about the author's view of the measures?", togri:"They are substantive responses to a real problem", chalg:["They are decorative only","They are too expensive to be useful","They are harmful"], izoh:"Muallif choralarni haqiqiy muammoni hal qiladigan jiddiy javob deb biladi."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-4", sarlavha:"Algorithms in hiring",
 matn:"Employers increasingly rely on algorithms to screen job applications, arguing that software can process thousands of CVs without the fatigue or unconscious bias that affect human reviewers. The argument has a certain appeal. Yet an algorithm trained on past hiring decisions learns to reproduce whatever patterns those decisions contained. If a company historically hired mostly men for technical roles, a model may quietly learn to penalise applications that mention women's colleges, without anyone having explicitly instructed it to do so.\n\nThe problem, therefore, is not that machines are malicious but that they are obedient. Auditing tools can detect some of these patterns, yet many systems remain opaque even to the companies that deploy them. Candidates rarely know why they were rejected, and regulators struggle to examine proprietary code. Automation may well make hiring faster; whether it makes hiring fairer depends on decisions that remain, for now, very much human.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What is the author's main claim?", togri:"Algorithms repeat past patterns, so fairness depends on human decisions", chalg:["Algorithms are malicious by design","Hiring should be fully automated","Companies never use algorithms"], izoh:"Matn oxirida adolatlilik inson qarorlariga bog'liq ekani aytilgan."},
  {s:"What does the word \"obedient\" imply about machines?", togri:"They follow patterns in their training data without judgement", chalg:["They refuse instructions","They choose their own goals","They ignore candidates completely"], izoh:"Muallif mashinalar o'quv ma'lumotlaridagi naqshlarga mulohazasiz ergashishini nazarda tutadi."},
  {s:"Why is the system described as opaque?", togri:"Candidates often don't know why they were rejected, and the code is proprietary", chalg:["It uses colour codes to rank people","It is written in Uzbek","It has no rules at all"], izoh:"Matnda nomzodlar sababni bilmasligi va kodning xususiy ekani sabab sifatida aytilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-5", sarlavha:"Museums and the question of return",
 matn:"For over a century, European and North American museums have displayed objects acquired during colonial expansion, often under conditions that would now be considered coercive. Defenders of these collections argue that the institutions preserved works that might otherwise have been damaged or lost, and that universal displays allow visitors from all backgrounds to encounter the world's cultures in one place. Critics reply that such arguments conveniently ignore how the objects arrived, and who was never asked for permission.\n\nRecent years have seen a gradual shift. Some governments have passed laws requiring museums to catalogue the origins of their holdings, and a few institutions have returned objects directly to communities that requested them. Such returns are rarely simple. Questions of ownership, of who speaks for a culture, and of whether a museum abroad can genuinely care for an object are all contested. Yet the debate has changed the terms on which museums must justify their presence, and that alone marks a significant transformation.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What do critics of the museum collections argue?", togri:"Defenders ignore how the objects were acquired", chalg:["The museums are too small","Visitors do not like art","The objects should be sold"], izoh:"Tanqidchilar himoyachilar buyumlar qanday olingani haqidagi savolni qulay tarzda chetlab o'tishini aytadi."},
  {s:"What does the phrase \"conveniently ignore\" suggest about the author?", togri:"The author is sceptical of the defenders' arguments", chalg:["The author fully agrees with the defenders","The author is neutral about colonialism","The author thinks museums are profitable"], izoh:"Muallif himoyachilar dalillarini tanqidiy baholaydi."},
  {s:"According to the text, what is the significance of the recent shift?", togri:"The terms on which museums must justify themselves have changed", chalg:["Museums have closed down","All objects have been returned","Visitor numbers have dropped"], izoh:"Matn oxirida muhokama muzeylar o'zini oqlashi kerak bo'lgan shartlarni o'zgartirgani aytilgan."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-6", sarlavha:"Oral tradition and written memory",
 matn:"Before writing became widespread, societies preserved their knowledge through memory, performance and repetition. Epic poets did not recite fixed texts; they reconstructed the story in each performance, adapting length, emphasis and detail to the audience in front of them. Scholars once assumed that such oral forms were inherently unstable, and that only writing could guarantee accuracy. Yet many oral traditions display remarkable consistency across generations, because their structures, formulas and rhythms make the content easier to remember.\n\nThe contrast between oral and written memory is therefore less sharp than it first appears. Written records, too, are copied, edited and reinterpreted, and a manuscript can accumulate errors as surely as a song changes in the mouths of successive singers. What differs is the relationship between the record and the reader. A written text can be consulted in silence, while an oral performance exists only in the moment of its telling and in the attention of those who hear it.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What did scholars once assume about oral traditions?", togri:"They were inherently unstable", chalg:["They were extremely accurate","They were meant only for children","They were always written down"], izoh:"Olimlar og'zaki shakllarni ichki beqaror deb o'ylagani aytilgan."},
  {s:"The author suggests that written records:", togri:"Can also accumulate errors and be reinterpreted", chalg:["Never change once written","Are always false","Have no readers"], izoh:"Muallif yozma matnlar ham ko'chiriladi, tahrirlanadi va xatolar to'planishi mumkinligini aytadi."},
  {s:"What main difference does the author identify?", togri:"A written text can be consulted in silence, while performance exists only in the moment", chalg:["Oral forms are much longer","Writing is cheaper to produce","Oral forms have no audience"], izoh:"Muallif yozma matnni jim o'qish va og'zaki ijroning faqat hozirgi paytda mavjudligini farq deb hisoblaydi."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-7", sarlavha:"The myth of the lone genius",
 matn:"Popular accounts of scientific discovery often feature a solitary genius who, through sudden insight, overturns the work of an entire field. The image is compelling, and it is largely misleading. Historians who examine laboratory notebooks and correspondence tend to find instead a dense web of collaborators, rivals, students and technicians, many of whom are rarely named. The famous moment of insight usually rests on years of groundwork laid by others, and on tools, funding and institutions that the genius did not personally create.\n\nWhy does the myth persist? Partly because a single name is easier to remember than a team, and partly because the narrative flatters a culture that prizes individual achievement. Yet the myth carries a cost. It can discourage those who do not see themselves as extraordinary, and it obscures the collective and often unglamorous labour on which progress depends. Recognising this does not diminish great scientists. It simply places them more accurately within the community that made their work possible.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What do historians typically find when they examine archives?", togri:"A dense web of collaborators", chalg:["A single author's name","Empty notebooks","Only rivals"], izoh:"Matnda tarixchilar hamkorlar, raqiblar, shogirdlar va texniklarning zich tarmog'ini topishi aytilgan."},
  {s:"Why does the myth persist, according to the text?", togri:"A single name is easier to remember, and the story flatters individualism", chalg:["Historians forbid collaboration","It has been scientifically proven","Funding agencies require it"], izoh:"Matnda ikki sabab aytilgan: bitta ismning osonligi va individual yutuqni maqtovchi madaniyat."},
  {s:"What is the author's view of the myth?", togri:"Critical, because it hides collective labour", chalg:["Admiring","Neutral","Unaware of the issue"], izoh:"Muallif afsona jamoaviy mehnatni yashirgani uchun uni tanqid qiladi."}
 ]},
{id:"c1-8", sarlavha:"The hidden price of cheap clothes",
 matn:"A garment that costs less than a sandwich is now a familiar feature of high-street shopping, and it would be easy to celebrate such affordability as a democratic achievement. The price, however, is not fully visible on the shelf. The rapid turnover of collections encourages consumers to buy more than they need, while the low prices depend on labour conditions and material choices that are rarely disclosed in full. The textile industry is among the most polluting in the world, and much of its waste ends up in landfills far from the shoppers who discarded the clothes.\n\nSome retailers have responded by promising recycling schemes and more durable lines. Their sincerity is difficult to judge from outside, and the language of sustainability has at times been used more to reassure than to reform. A more fundamental change would require producers to bear the costs of disposal, and consumers to reconsider what counts as a bargain. Until then, cheapness will continue to be measured in the wrong currency.",
 savollar:[
  {s:"What is the hidden cost of fast fashion, according to the text?", togri:"Labour conditions, pollution and waste that are not visible on the shelf", chalg:["Taxes on clothing","Shipping costs only","Shop rents"], izoh:"Matnda mehnat sharoitlari, ifloslanish va chiqindi tokchada ko'rinmasligi aytilgan."},
  {s:"What does the author suggest about the language of sustainability?", togri:"It may be used more to reassure than to reform", chalg:["It is always sincere","It is illegal in most countries","It has no effect at all"], izoh:"Muallif barqarorlik tilining ba'zan islohot emas, tinchlantirish uchun ishlatilishini aytadi."},
  {s:"The phrase \"measured in the wrong currency\" most likely means:", togri:"Value is judged by price alone, ignoring social and environmental costs", chalg:["Prices are shown in foreign money","Exchange rates are wrong","Clothes are paid for with coins"], izoh:"Ibora qiymatning faqat narx bilan baholanib, ijtimoiy va ekologik xarajatlar e'tiborga olinmasligini anglatadi."}
 ]}
]
};

/* ── YOZISH: ko'nikma darajalari bo'yicha namunalar va IELTS namunalari ── */
const YOZISH_DB = {
"darajalar":[
 {daraja:"A1", mavzu:"Men haqimda", matn:"My name is Dilnoza. I am 20 years old. I live in Tashkent with my mother, my father and my little brother. My father is a doctor. My mother is a teacher. I like music and I play the guitar. I have one cat. It is black and very lazy. I am a student at university. I study English every day.", izoh:["Present simple: am, is, have, play, study","Oddiy gaplar: ega + kesim + to'ldiruvchi","Egalik olmoshlari: my, his, her, its","Ko'p gaplar bir xil tuzilishda, bu A1 uchun to'g'ri"]},
 {daraja:"A2", mavzu:"Mening shaharim", matn:"Tashkent is a big and beautiful city. It has many parks, wide streets and old buildings. I like to walk in Amir Temur Square in the evening because it is quiet and there are many people. There are a lot of good restaurants, but my favourite is a small place near my house. The food is cheap and delicious. The only problem is the traffic. In the morning, the roads are very busy, so I take the metro to work.", izoh:["There is / There are, a lot of","Sabab va natija: because, so","Qarama-qarshilik: but","Sifatlar: big, beautiful, cheap, delicious"]},
 {daraja:"B1", mavzu:"Forma kiyish kerakmi?", matn:"Some people think that students should wear school uniforms. I agree with this idea for two main reasons. First, uniforms make everyone look equal, so students from poor families do not feel embarrassed about their clothes. Second, students spend less time in the morning deciding what to wear. However, some students argue that uniforms limit self-expression. This is a fair point, but schools can still allow small personal choices, such as bags or hairstyles. In conclusion, I believe uniforms bring more benefits than problems.", izoh:["Fikr bildirish: I agree with this idea","Sabablar tartibi: First, Second","Qarshi fikrni tan olish: However, This is a fair point","Xulosa: In conclusion, I believe"]},
 {daraja:"B2", mavzu:"Masofaviy ta'lim", matn:"Remote learning is often criticised for isolating students, but I believe its advantages outweigh this drawback. Firstly, it gives learners flexibility: those who work during the day can study in the evening, and students in remote regions gain access to teachers they would never otherwise meet. Secondly, recorded lessons allow students to pause and rewatch difficult explanations, which is hard in a crowded classroom. It is true that some students lose motivation without a routine. Nevertheless, this problem can be reduced through regular video meetings and clear deadlines. Therefore, when well organised, remote learning can be at least as effective as traditional classes.", izoh:["Ehtiyotkor fikr: I believe, It is true that","Murakkab gap: those who ..., and students ...","Bog'lovchilar: Firstly, Secondly, Nevertheless, Therefore","Qarshi dalilga javob va xulosa"]},
 {daraja:"C1", mavzu:"Texnologik taraqqiyot", matn:"The claim that technological progress inevitably improves human wellbeing rests on an appealing but incomplete assumption: that every innovation distributes its benefits and burdens fairly. History suggests otherwise. The mechanisation of agriculture, for instance, multiplied output, yet it also displaced millions of rural workers whose livelihoods could not easily be transferred to new industries. To say that society was ultimately enriched is not to deny that many individuals were left worse off in the interim. What matters, then, is not whether progress occurs but who bears its costs during the transition, and whether institutions exist to cushion those who pay the price.", izoh:["Nozik fikr: rests on an appealing but incomplete assumption","Hedging: to say that ... is not to deny that","Murakkab tuzilma: could not easily be transferred","Muhokama yakunida savol: What matters, then, is ..."]}
],
"ielts":[
 {tur:"IELTS Writing Task 1 (Academic) — chiziqli grafik", mavzu:"Energiya manbalari, 2000–2020", savol:"The line graph shows the share of electricity generated from coal, gas and renewable sources in Country X between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features. Ma'lumot (grafik o'rniga): 2000 — ko'mir 70%, qayta tiklanuvchi 5%, gaz 25%; 2020 — ko'mir 35%, qayta tiklanuvchi 30%, gaz 20%.", matn:"The line graph illustrates changes in the share of electricity generation from three sources in Country X between 2000 and 2020. Overall, coal's dominance declined markedly, while renewable energy expanded rapidly over the period. In 2000, coal accounted for approximately 70% of electricity generation, but this proportion fell steadily, reaching around 35% by 2020. By contrast, renewable sources, which include solar, wind and hydro power, rose from only 5% at the start of the period to 30% at the end. Their growth was particularly rapid after 2008, and by 2012 they had drawn level with gas. Gas, meanwhile, began at about 25% and declined gradually to around 20%, remaining a stable but less significant source. The decline in coal was steady rather than sudden, with no single year showing a dramatic drop. Overall, renewables overtook gas in the following years, and by the end of the period the three sources made up a far more balanced energy mix than at the start.", baho:"Taxminiy Band 7", izoh:["Umumiy tendensiya (Overall) yozilgan — bu baho uchun muhim","Aniq raqamlar va davrlar berilgan, lekin hamma raqam yod olinmagan","Asosiy o'zgarishlar tanlab keltirilgan, ortiqcha tafsilotsiz","Sinonimlar: illustrates, dominance, drawn level with, overtook"]},
 {tur:"IELTS Writing Task 2 (Essay) — jamoat transporti", mavzu:"Hukumat nimaga sarflashi kerak?", savol:"Some people believe that governments should invest in public transport rather than building new roads. To what extent do you agree or disagree?", matn:"Traffic congestion has become a defining problem of modern cities, and governments face a genuine dilemma about how to respond. Building new roads may appear to be the most direct solution, yet experience suggests that additional capacity often attracts additional drivers, and congestion returns within a few years. For this reason, I largely agree that public investment should be prioritised.\n\nThe main advantage of public transport is its capacity. A single metro line can move tens of thousands of passengers an hour, a volume that would require several motorway lanes to match. Buses and trams also offer affordable mobility to people who cannot drive, including students, older residents and those on low incomes. Furthermore, a reliable network encourages people to use it regularly, which in turn makes frequent services financially viable. Moreover, reducing private car use lowers emissions and improves air quality, which benefits everyone, including those who never use public transport themselves.\n\nNevertheless, roads are not entirely unnecessary. Freight, emergency services and rural communities often depend on road infrastructure, and a city that neglects its roads will struggle economically. The sensible approach, therefore, is not to choose one over the other, but to maintain existing roads while directing new funding towards rail, bus rapid transit and safe cycling routes. In addition, a well-designed bus network can serve areas that rail will never reach, so the two systems complement rather than compete with each other.\n\nIn conclusion, although roads remain important, governments should give greater priority to public transport, because it offers more efficient, equitable and sustainable mobility.", baho:"Taxminiy Band 7–7.5", izoh:["Kirish: muammo tanish qilinadi va pozitsiya aniq (I largely agree)","Har bir body paragraf bitta asosiy g'oyaga ega va dalil bilan ta'minlangan","Qarshi dalil (roads are not entirely unnecessary) va muvozanatli xulosa","Bog'lovchilar tabiiy ishlatilgan: Moreover, Nevertheless, therefore"]}
]
};

/* ── MATNLAR VA YOZISH: sahifa ── */
let joriyMatnTab='matnlar', joriyMatnDaraja='B2', joriyMatnOchiq=null;
function matnlarKo_rsatish(){
  if(joriyMatnTab==='yozish') return yozishKo_rsatish();
  const ro=MATNLAR_DB[joriyMatnDaraja]||[];
  const tugmalar=[['B1',"O'rta"],['B2',"Yuqori-o'rta"],['C1',"Ilg'or"]].map(([d,nom])=>
    `<button class="daraja-tugma ${joriyMatnDaraja===d?'tanlangan':''}" onclick="matnDarajaTanlash('${d}')">${d} · ${nom}</button>`).join('');
  document.getElementById('kontent-joy').innerHTML=`
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2>Matnlar</h2><p>Ingliz tilidagi asl matnni o'qing, so'ng savollarga javob bering. Har bir savoldan keyin izoh chiqadi.</p></div>
    <div class="daraja-filter">${tugmalar}</div>
    <div class="grammatika-royxat" style="margin-top:14px;">
      ${ro.map((m,i)=>matnKartaHTML(m,i)).join('')}
    </div>`;
}
function matnDarajaTanlash(d){ joriyMatnDaraja=d; joriyMatnOchiq=null; matnlarKo_rsatish(); }
function matnToglash(id){ joriyMatnOchiq = joriyMatnOchiq===id ? null : id; matnlarKo_rsatish(); }
function matnKartaHTML(m,i){
  const ochiq = joriyMatnOchiq===m.id;
  const javoblar = (PROGRESS.matnJavob||{});
  const yechilgan = m.savollar.filter((_,qi)=>javoblar[m.id+'_'+qi]).length;
  const sana = m.matn.split(/\s+/).length;
  return `<div class="grammatika-element">
    <div class="grammatika-bosh" onclick="matnToglash('${m.id}')">
      <span class="unit-raqam">${i+1}</span>
      <span style="flex:1;">${m.sarlavha}</span>
      <span class="belgi" style="flex-shrink:0;">${yechilgan}/${m.savollar.length}</span>
      <span>${ochiq?'▲':'▼'}</span>
    </div>
    ${ochiq?`<div class="grammatika-tana ochiq">
      <div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--kulrang);margin-bottom:10px;">📄 ${sana} so'z · ${m.savollar.length} ta savol</div>
      <div class="qoida-blok" style="white-space:pre-line;font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;">${m.matn}</div>
      <div style="margin-top:18px;">
        <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--sariq);margin-bottom:10px;">❓ Savollar — matn asosida javob bering</div>
        ${m.savollar.map((q,qi)=>matnSavolHTML(m,qi,q)).join('')}
      </div>
    </div>`:''}
  </div>`;
}
function matnSavolHTML(m,qi,q){
  const key=m.id+'_'+qi;
  const tartib = matnTartibi(key);
  const variantlar = tartib.map(j=>[q.togri,...q.chalg][j]);
  const javob = (PROGRESS.matnJavob||{})[key];
  const togriIdx = tartib.indexOf(0);
  return `<div style="margin-bottom:14px;padding:14px;background:#071220;border-radius:10px;">
    <div style="font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:10px;">${qi+1}. ${q.s}</div>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px;">
      ${variantlar.map((v,oi)=>{
        let cls='test-variant';
        if(javob){ if(oi===togriIdx) cls+=' togri'; else if(oi===javob.oi && !javob.ok) cls+=' noto-gri'; }
        return `<button class="${cls}" ${javob?'disabled':''} onclick="matnJavob('${m.id}',${qi},${oi})">${v}</button>`;
      }).join('')}
    </div>
    ${javob?`<div class="test-fikr ${javob.ok?'yaxshi':'yomon'}" style="margin-top:10px;">${javob.ok?"✅ To'g'ri! +3 XP":"❌ Noto'g'ri."} ${q.izoh}</div>`:''}
  </div>`;
}
function matnTartibi(key){
  PROGRESS.matnVar = PROGRESS.matnVar || {};
  if(!PROGRESS.matnVar[key]){
    const t=[0,1,2,3];
    for(let i=t.length-1;i>0;i--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[t[i],t[j]]=[t[j],t[i]];}
    PROGRESS.matnVar[key]=t; progressSaqlash(PROGRESS);
  }
  return PROGRESS.matnVar[key];
}
function matnJavob(id,qi,oi){
  const m=Object.values(MATNLAR_DB).flat().find(x=>x.id===id);
  if(!m) return;
  const key=id+'_'+qi;
  PROGRESS.matnJavob = PROGRESS.matnJavob || {};
  if(PROGRESS.matnJavob[key]) return;
  const tartib=matnTartibi(key);
  const ok = tartib[oi]===0;
  PROGRESS.matnJavob[key]={oi,ok};
  progressSaqlash(PROGRESS);
  if(ok) xpQoshish(3);
  kunlikQayd(0,1,ok?1:0);
  matnlarKo_rsatish();
}

/* YOZISH */
function yozishKo_rsatish(){
  const dar=YOZISH_DB.darajalar.map(d=>`
    <div class="test-karta" style="text-align:left;margin-bottom:14px;">
      <div class="qator" style="margin-bottom:10px;"><span class="belgi">${d.daraja}</span><strong>${d.mavzu}</strong></div>
      <div class="qoida-blok" style="white-space:pre-line;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;">${d.matn}</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--sariq);margin:12px 0 6px;">🧭 Nima uchun bu ${d.daraja}?</div>
      <ul style="margin:0 0 0 18px;color:var(--kulrang);font-size:13px;line-height:1.75;">${d.izoh.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul>
    </div>`).join('');
  const ie=YOZISH_DB.ielts.map(t=>`
    <div class="test-karta" style="text-align:left;margin-bottom:14px;">
      <div class="qator" style="margin-bottom:10px;"><span class="belgi">${t.baho}</span><strong style="font-size:14px;">${t.tur}</strong></div>
      <div style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--kulrang);margin-bottom:8px;">Mavzu: ${t.mavzu}</div>
      <div class="qoida-blok" style="font-size:13px;"><strong>📋 Topshiriq:</strong><br>${t.savol}</div>
      <div class="qoida-blok" style="white-space:pre-line;font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;margin-top:10px;"><strong>✍️ Namuna javob:</strong>\n\n${t.matn}</div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:var(--sariq);margin:12px 0 6px;">🔎 Nega bu yuqori baho?</div>
      <ul style="margin:0 0 0 18px;color:var(--kulrang);font-size:13px;line-height:1.75;">${t.izoh.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join('')}</ul>
    </div>`).join('');
  document.getElementById('kontent-joy').innerHTML=`
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2>Yozish</h2><p>Ko'nikma darajalari bo'yicha namunalar (A1–C1) va IELTS Writing namunalari. Har bir namunadan oldin nimaga e'tibor berish kerakligi yozilgan.</p></div>
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2 style="font-size:17px;">Ko'nikma darajalari bo'yicha</h2></div>
    ${dar}
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2 style="font-size:17px;">Foydali iboralar</h2><p>Har bir darajada yozishda ishlatsa bo'ladigan tayyor iboralar. Ularni o'z gapingizda qo'llab ko'ring.</p></div>
    ${Object.keys(YOZISH_DB.iboralar).map(d=>`<div class="test-karta" style="text-align:left;margin-bottom:12px;"><div class="qator" style="margin-bottom:8px;"><span class="belgi">${d}</span></div>${YOZISH_DB.iboralar[d].map(([en,uz])=>`<div style="padding:6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--chegara);"><div style="font-size:14px;">${en}</div><div style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--kulrang);">${uz}</div></div>`).join('')}</div>`).join('')}
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2 style="font-size:17px;">IELTS Writing namunalari</h2><p>Baholar taxminiy. Rasmiy baholash IELTS mezonlari bo'yicha, lekin bu namunalar yo'nalish beradi.</p></div>
    ${ie}`;
}

/* ── MORFOLOGIYA (B1): so'z yasalishi — qo'shimchalar va mashq ── */
const MORF_B1 = [
 {qism:"-er", ma:"fe'ldan ot: bajaruvchi shaxs", qoida:"Fe'l oxiriga -er qo'shilib, uni bajaruvchi shaxs yoki vosita ma'nosidagi otga aylantiradi.", misollar:[["teach","teacher","o'qituvchi"],["work","worker","ishchi"],["write","writer","yozuvchi"],["drive","driver","haydovchi"]]},
 {qism:"-ment", ma:"fe'ldan ot: harakat natijasi", qoida:"Fe'l oxiriga -ment qo'shilib, harakatning natijasi yoki jarayonini bildiruvchi otni hosil qiladi.", misollar:[["develop","development","rivojlanish"],["enjoy","enjoyment","zavq"],["move","movement","harakat"],["agree","agreement","kelishuv"]]},
 {qism:"-ness", ma:"sifatdan ot: holat, daraja", qoida:"Sifat oxiriga -ness qo'shilib, shu sifatning holati yoki darajasini bildiruvchi otni hosil qiladi.", misollar:[["happy","happiness","baxt"],["kind","kindness","mehribonlik"],["dark","darkness","qorong'ulik"],["ill","illness","kasallik"]]},
 {qism:"-ful", ma:"otdan sifat: to'la, ega", qoida:"Ot oxiriga -ful qo'shilib, 'shunga to'la' yoki 'shunga ega' ma'nosidagi sifatni hosil qiladi.", misollar:[["help","helpful","foydali, yordam beradigan"],["care","careful","ehtiyotkor"],["beauty","beautiful","chiroyli"],["use","useful","foydali"]]},
 {qism:"-less", ma:"otdan sifat: yo'q", qoida:"Ot oxiriga -less qo'shilib, 'shunga ega emas' ma'nosini beradi. Bu -ful ning teskarisi.", misollar:[["care","careless","beparvo"],["hope","hopeless","umidsiz"],["use","useless","foydasiz"],["fear","fearless","qo'rqmas"]]},
 {qism:"-able", ma:"fe'ldan sifat: mumkin", qoida:"Fe'l oxiriga -able qo'shilib, 'qilish mumkin' ma'nosidagi sifatni hosil qiladi.", misollar:[["understand","understandable","tushunarli"],["read","readable","o'qish mumkin"],["wash","washable","yuvsa bo'ladigan"],["comfort","comfortable","qulay"]]},
 {qism:"-ly", ma:"sifatdan ravish: qanday qilib", qoida:"Sifat oxiriga -ly qo'shilib ravish hosil qiladi. Eslatma: y bilan tugagan sifatda y → ily (happy → happily).", misollar:[["quick","quickly","tez"],["careful","carefully","ehtiyotkorlik bilan"],["sudden","suddenly","to'satdan"],["happy","happily","baxtli holda"]]}
];
let MORF_JAVOB = {};
let MORF_SAVOLLAR = [];
function morfYangiMashq(){
  const tartib = aralashtir(MORF_B1.map((_,i)=>i));
  MORF_SAVOLLAR = tartib.map(ci=>{
    const cat = MORF_B1[ci];
    const [asos, togri, uz] = aralashtir(cat.misollar)[0];
    const chalg = MORF_B1.filter((_,j)=>j!==ci).flatMap(c=>c.misollar.map(x=>x[1]));
    const variantlar = aralashtir([togri, ...aralashtir(chalg).slice(0,3)]);
    return {qism:cat.qism, asos, uz, togri, variantlar};
  });
  MORF_JAVOB = {};
  morfologiyaKo_rsatish();
}
function morfJavob(i,oi){
  if(MORF_JAVOB[i]!==undefined) return;
  const q=MORF_SAVOLLAR[i];
  const ok = q.variantlar[oi]===q.togri;
  MORF_JAVOB[i]={oi,ok};
  if(ok) xpQoshish(2);
  kunlikQayd(0,1,ok?1:0);
  morfologiyaKo_rsatish();
}
function morfologiyaKo_rsatish(){
  if(!MORF_SAVOLLAR.length) { MORF_SAVOLLAR=[]; }
  const qoidalar = MORF_B1.map(c=>`
    <div class="grammatika-element" style="padding:14px 16px;">
      <div class="qator" style="margin-bottom:8px;"><span class="belgi">${c.qism}</span><strong style="font-size:14px;">${c.ma}</strong></div>
      <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--kulrang);margin-bottom:10px;line-height:1.6;">${c.qoida}</div>
      <div class="qator" style="gap:6px;">${c.misollar.map(([a,b,u])=>`<span class="belgi">${a} → <strong>${b}</strong> <span style="color:var(--kulrang);">(${u})</span></span>`).join('')}</div>
    </div>`).join('');
  const savollar = MORF_SAVOLLAR.map((q,i)=>{
    const j=MORF_JAVOB[i];
    return `<div style="margin-bottom:14px;padding:14px;background:#071220;border-radius:10px;">
      <div style="font-size:13.5px;margin-bottom:10px;">${i+1}. <strong>${q.asos}</strong> so'zidan <strong>${q.qism}</strong> qo'shimchasi bilan so'z yasang <span style="color:var(--kulrang);">(${q.uz})</span></div>
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:7px;">
        ${q.variantlar.map((v,oi)=>{
          let cls='test-variant';
          if(j){ if(v===q.togri) cls+=' togri'; else if(oi===j.oi && !j.ok) cls+=' noto-gri'; }
          return `<button class="${cls}" ${j?'disabled':''} onclick="morfJavob(${i},${oi})">${v}</button>`;
        }).join('')}
      </div>
      ${j?`<div class="test-fikr ${j.ok?'yaxshi':'yomon'}" style="margin-top:10px;">${j.ok?"✅ To'g'ri!":"❌ Noto'g'ri."} ${q.asos} → ${q.togri} (${q.uz})</div>`:''}
    </div>`;}).join('');
  document.getElementById('kontent-joy').innerHTML=`
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2>So'z yasalishi</h2><p>Qo'shimchalar yordamida so'zlar oilasini tushuning: bitta so'zdan bir nechta yangi so'z yasaladi. B1 darajasi.</p></div>
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha"><h2 style="font-size:17px;">Qo'shimchalar</h2></div>
    ${qoidalar}
    <div class="sahifa-sarlavha" style="margin-top:20px;"><h2 style="font-size:17px;">🎯 Mashq</h2><p>Har bir savolda asos so'z va qo'shimcha berilgan. To'g'ri yasalgan so'zni tanlang.</p></div>
    ${MORF_SAVOLLAR.length?savollar:''}
    <div style="text-align:center;margin-top:10px;"><button class="test-keyingi" onclick="morfYangiMashq()">🎲 ${MORF_SAVOLLAR.length?'Yangi mashq':'Mashqni boshlash'}</button></div>`;
}

/* ── YOZISH: 2-to'plam qo'shimcha namunalar va foydali iboralar ── */
YOZISH_DB.darajalar.push(
 {daraja:"A1", mavzu:"Do'stimga xat (email)", matn:"Hi Sara,\n\nHow are you? I am fine. I am in Samarkand now with my family. The city is very old and beautiful. We visit the Registan every day. The food is good, and I like the bread here very much.\n\nPlease write to me soon.\n\nSee you,\nAziza", izoh:["Salomlashish va xayrlashish: Hi / See you","Present simple va present continuous: I am in, We visit","Oddiy, qisqa gaplar; har bir gap bitta fikr","very much, very old — kuchaytiruvchi so'zlar"]},
 {daraja:"A2", mavzu:"Dam olish kunim (hikoya)", matn:"Last Saturday I went to the mountains with my friends. We left home at seven in the morning. The road was long, but the view was amazing. We walked for three hours and had a picnic near a river. In the afternoon it started to rain, so we went back to the car quickly. It was a tiring day, but I really enjoyed it.", izoh:["O'tgan oddiy zamon: went, left, had, walked","Vaqt ketma-ketligi: in the morning, after, in the afternoon","Sabab va qarama-qarshilik: so, but","really enjoyed — his bildiruvchi so'z"]},
 {daraja:"B1", mavzu:"Rasmiy shikoyat xati", matn:"Dear Mr Karimov,\n\nI am writing to complain about the delivery of my order (No. 4521). It was supposed to arrive on 5 May, but it has not come yet. I called customer service twice, but nobody answered. I would be grateful if you could check the status of my order and let me know when it will be delivered. If the problem is not solved by Friday, I will ask for a full refund.\n\nYours sincerely,\nNodira Rahimova", izoh:["Rasmiy xat: Dear Mr ... / Yours sincerely","I am writing to complain — maqsadni aniq aytish","be supposed to — kutilgan narsa va haqiqat","I would be grateful if ... — muloyim so'rov; If ... will ... — shart"]},
 {daraja:"B2", mavzu:"Restoran sharhi (review)", matn:"The new place near the river is far better than I expected. The atmosphere is calm and the staff are friendly, though the service can be slow at weekends. The main dishes are generous and beautifully presented. The lamb was the best I have had in the city, although the desserts are less impressive than the starters. Compared with other restaurants in the area, the prices are fair. I would definitely recommend it, especially for a quiet dinner, but I would book in advance.", izoh:["Taqqoslash: better than, compared with, less impressive than","Qarama-qarshilik va cheklov: though, although","Kuchaytirilgan baho: far better, definitely recommend","Ob-havo emas, his: calm, generous, beautifully presented"]},
 {daraja:"C1", mavzu:"Shaxsiy mulohaza (tahliliy)", matn:"Looking back, I am struck by how little I understood about failure at the start of my career. I had assumed that setbacks were signs of incompetence; only later did I recognise them as the raw material of learning. Rarely have I been so mistaken about a single idea. Had I not been forced to rethink my approach, I would probably still be repeating the same errors, convinced of my own competence. The lesson, if there is one, is not that failure is pleasant, but that it is informative.", izoh:["Inversiya: Only later did I ..., Rarely have I ..., Had I not been ...","Nozik baho: little I understood, the raw material of learning","Murakkab gap va xulosa: The lesson, if there is one, ...","Mavzu uchun aniq so'zlar: setbacks, incompetence, convinced"]}
);
YOZISH_DB.ielts.push(
 {tur:"IELTS Writing Task 1 (Academic) — jarayon diagrammasi", mavzu:"Qog'ozni qayta ishlash zavodda", savol:"The diagram shows the stages in recycling waste paper at a factory. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features.", matn:"The diagram illustrates the stages involved in recycling waste paper at a factory. Overall, the process consists of six main stages, beginning with collection and ending with the production of new paper rolls.\n\nInitially, used paper is collected from homes, offices and schools, and then transported to the factory. Once there, it is sorted by type and freed of contaminants such as plastic and metal. The sorted paper is then placed into a large pulper, where it is mixed with water and broken down into fibres. The entire process is designed to minimise waste, and the recovered fibres can be reused several times before they become too short.\n\nNext, the mixture is cleaned to remove ink and any remaining particles. After cleaning, the pulp is spread onto a flat screen and pressed to remove water. Finally, the sheets are dried, rolled and stored, ready for sale to printing companies. Throughout the process, water is recycled, which reduces the factory's overall consumption.", baho:"Taxminiy Band 7", izoh:["Overall bilan umumiy jarayon tavsiflangan","Jarayon passive (is collected, is sorted) bilan yoziladi — diagrammaga mos","Ketma-ketlik: Initially, Once there, Next, After cleaning, Finally","Ortiqcha detal yo'q: har bir bosqich bir gapda"]},
 {tur:"IELTS Writing Task 2 (Essay) — ikki qarash", mavzu:"Maktabda amaliy yoki akademik fanlar", savol:"Some people believe that schools should teach practical skills such as cooking and budgeting, while others think that academic subjects should remain the main priority. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.", matn:"Education systems around the world are often criticised for focusing too heavily on theory. Some argue that schools should prioritise practical life skills, while others maintain that academic subjects must remain at the centre of the curriculum. Both positions have merit, but I believe that a balanced approach offers the greatest benefit.\n\nThose who favour practical skills point out that many young people leave school unable to cook a healthy meal, manage a bank account or understand a tenancy agreement. Such knowledge affects everyday wellbeing and independence far more directly than, for example, memorising historical dates. This is particularly important in a world where debt is easy to accumulate. Teaching these skills early could therefore reduce financial problems and help students become responsible adults.\n\nOn the other hand, supporters of academic subjects argue that schools exist to develop critical thinking, language and scientific reasoning, which underpin almost every profession. Mathematics and literacy, in particular, provide the foundation for lifelong learning. Without them, students would struggle to acquire practical skills in the first place, let alone adapt to new technologies. Furthermore, a student who understands how an argument is structured is often better at analysing a business contract.\n\nIn my view, the two goals are not mutually exclusive. Schools could maintain a strong academic core while integrating practical modules, such as budgeting projects in mathematics lessons or cooking linked to chemistry. In conclusion, a curriculum that combines both approaches would prepare students more effectively for real life. Ultimately, education should equip young people not only for a first job but also for the decisions they will face throughout their lives.", baho:"Taxminiy Band 7–7.5", izoh:["Ikkala qarash alohida paragrafda, teng muomala bilan","Har bir dalil misol bilan: many young people leave school unable to ...","Muvozanatli xulosa va o'z fikri (In my view)","Bog'lovchilar: On the other hand, Furthermore, therefore, in particular"]}
);
YOZISH_DB.iboralar = {
 "A1":[["My name is ...","Mening ismim ..."],["I like ...","Men ... yoqtiraman"],["I think that ...","Menimcha, ..."],["Thank you for ...","... uchun rahmat"],["See you soon.","Tez orada ko'rishguncha."]],
 "A2":[["Last week I ...","O'tgan hafta men ..."],["First, ... Then, ... Finally, ...","Avval, ... So'ng, ... Nihoyat, ..."],["I'd like to ...","Men ... qilmoqchiman"],["It was ... because ...","... chunki ..."],["I really enjoyed ...","Menga ... juda yoqdi"]],
 "B1":[["I am writing to ...","... haqida yozyapman"],["I would be grateful if ...","... qilsangiz minnatdor bo'laman"],["On the one hand ..., on the other hand ...","Bir tomondan ..., ikkinchi tomondan ..."],["In my opinion, ...","Menimcha, ..."],["As a result, ...","Natijada, ..."]],
 "B2":[["It is widely believed that ...","Ko'pchilik ... deb hisoblaydi"],["This suggests that ...","Bu shuni ko'rsatadiki, ..."],["However, ... / Moreover, ...","Biroq, ... / Bundan tashqari, ..."],["There is no doubt that ...","Shubhasiz, ..."],["Compared with ..., ...","... bilan solishtirganda, ..."]],
 "C1":[["It could be argued that ...","Shunday deb aytish mumkinki, ..."],["Far from ..., it ...","... dan yiroq, u ..."],["Given that ..., ...","... ekanligini hisobga olsak, ..."],["This raises the question of ...","Bu ... masalasini tug'diradi"],["Only in this way can ...","Faqat shu yo'l bilan ..."]]
};

/* BOSHLASH */
[['sozlikKo_rsatish','sozlik'],['kartochaYangiBoshlash','kartochkalar'],['testYangiBoshlash','test'],['oyinlarKo_rsatish','oyinlar'],['grammatikaKo_rsatish','grammatika'],['taraqqiyotKo_rsatish','taraqqiyot']].forEach(([fn,mod])=>{
  const asl=window[fn]; if(typeof asl!=='function') return;
  window[fn]=function(){ bolimBelgila(mod); return asl.apply(this,arguments); };
});
modulAlmashtir('sozlik');
document.getElementById('streakYonPanel').textContent = `🔥 ${PROGRESS.streak.joriy} kunlik seriya`;
xpKorsatkichniYangilash();
</script>
</body>
</html>
